=== HINT 1: Convert thread + create fibers ===
g_scheduler_fiber = ConvertThreadToFiber(NULL);

for (int i = 0; i < NUM_FIBERS; i++)
    g_fibers[i] = CreateFiber(0, fiber_fn, &ctxs[i]);

=== HINT 2: Yield inside fiber_fn ===
SwitchToFiber(g_scheduler_fiber);   // suspend me, resume scheduler

=== HINT 3: Scheduler dispatches ===
SwitchToFiber(g_fibers[i]);   // resume fiber i; returns when it yields

=== HINT 4: Scheduler loop ===
// The loop in main already handles remaining count.
// Just add SwitchToFiber(g_fibers[i]) in the if(!g_done[i]) block.

=== KEY INSIGHT ===
SwitchToFiber() does NOT go to the OS - it's a pure user-mode context switch
(saves/restores registers, stack pointer, FPU state).
Cost ≈ 50-100 ns vs ~1000 ns for a thread context switch.
Fibers are single-threaded cooperative: only one runs at a time per OS thread.
This is the foundation of "green threads" / coroutine schedulers.
